WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.470
- [Narrator] If it seems like the US

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.630
is constantly about to hit the debt limit,

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:06.600
also known as the debt
ceiling, it's because it is.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.370
- It's kind of like the horror film

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.260
that Hollywood keeps making sequels of,

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.600
even though no one liked the first one,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.180
- [Narrator] Every few
years or sometimes months,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.460
Congress needs to vote to raise it

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.620
and they will need to
do it again some time

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.220
in 2023 when Congress will look like this,

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.980
a Republican-held House and
a Democratic-held Senate

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.990
with a Democrat in the White House.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.330
And Republicans plan on
using the debt ceiling

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.240
as leverage to negotiate spending cuts.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.310
- The debt ceiling needs to be lifted.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.890
But wouldn't it be a normal thing

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.590
if we're $31 trillion in debt

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.330
not to just give a blank check?

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.520
- [Narrator] So while Congress
still looks like this,

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.830
Democrats are looking to
act on the debt ceiling

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.950
so it's not something
Republicans can use next year.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.110
Here are three things they might do

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.763
in that lame-duck session.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.730
But first, a brief history lesson

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.450
from what happened another
time Congress looked like this.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.520
The debt limit was raised many times

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.010
through recent history without much debate

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.490
until 2011 when Republicans
decided to use it

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.790
as a bargaining chip to
negotiate for more spending cuts.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.890
- The American people will
not accept an increase

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:14.723
in the debt limit

00:01:14.723 --> 00:01:18.030
without significant
spending cuts and reforms.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.290
- [Narrator] The three sides debated

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.980
as the US got closer and
closer to defaulting until.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.690
- [Reporter 1] Congressional
leaders reached a deal

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:25.523
with the president.

00:01:25.523 --> 00:01:27.330
- [Reporter 2] Just one
day before Uncle Sam

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:28.590
hit his credit limit.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:29.970
- [Narrator] The US didn't default,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.130
but the country's perfect AAA rating

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.730
was downgraded by S&amp;P in
part because of the fight.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.860
- We're no longer the gold standard

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.660
as it relates to the world's economy.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.330
- Longer term, we may see
a rise in borrowing costs.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.730
- If history repeats itself,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.310
that is a scary scenario
for this government.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.230
- The US can't default on its debt.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.990
That would have huge implications
for the economy worldwide.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:52.950
Even just getting close

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.430
to the debt limit can cause
big problems for the economy.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.290
- [Narrator] So to
avoid a fight next year,

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.350
Democrats this year have three options.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.400
The first is to just get rid
of the debt limit altogether.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.110
The US is one of two countries

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.270
that even have one in the first place,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.730
and there's already legislation
introduced to make it

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.890
like all these other countries.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.540
So the Treasury could just take on

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.790
as much debt as it needs
to pay for spending

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.400
that Congress already voted for.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.040
Treasury Secretary Janet
Yellen was asked last year

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.570
if she'd support the repeal.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.403
- Yes, I would.

00:02:25.403 --> 00:02:30.403
I believe it's very destructive
to put the president

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.780
and myself, the Treasury Secretary,

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.350
in a situation where we might be unable

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:41.880
to pay the bills that result
from those past decisions.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.740
- [Narrator] But Biden
says he wouldn't sign it.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.624
- You mean just say we
don't have a debt limit?

00:02:45.624 --> 00:02:46.680
- [Reporter 3] No debt limit.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:47.730
- No.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.980
- [Narrator] So that's the
least likely possibility.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.010
The second is to just raise
the debt limit so high

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.900
it's not really a problem.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.040
That's what the other country

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.050
with a debt limit, Denmark, does.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:01.770
Its debt would have to quadruple
to reach its current limit.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.830
The likely plan wouldn't be
to raise the US's that high

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.210
but just enough to remove it

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.660
as something that would
need to be debated in 2023.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:10.800
- It's not due till next year

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.160
but the Democrats could
pass something potentially

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.420
with Republican votes also

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.280
in the Senate that would raise it.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.253
So we wouldn't have to worry
about it say till 2024, 2025.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:22.500
- [Narrator] But the most likely plan

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.050
is to restructure the way
the debt ceiling is raised

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.530
in the first place and make
it not Congress's problem.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.590
The idea originates from that 2011 battle.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.000
- It was an idea that was brought forward

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.330
by now Senate Minority
Leader Mitch McConnell

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.260
who at the time was looking

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.630
for ways to help his
members avoid having to vote

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:40.560
for the debt ceiling,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.480
but give them a way to vote against it.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.190
- [Narrator] Currently,
a majority in the House

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.390
and the Senate have to vote

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.210
for raising the debt
ceiling and then it's signed

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:51.120
or vetoed by the
president, like most bills.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.820
But members of Congress,
especially Republicans,

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.820
don't like having to vote
for raising the debt ceiling.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.890
So what several bipartisan plans would do

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.260
is essentially reverse this.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.850
It would give the executive branch,

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.310
so the president or even
the treasury secretary

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.710
the authority to raise the debt limit

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.340
and Congress could pass a joint
resolution of disapproval.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.720
- Basically, Congress instead

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.856
of voting to affirmatively
raise the debt ceiling,

00:04:15.856 --> 00:04:18.450
members of Congress could vote basically

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.160
against raising the debt ceiling.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.010
- [Narrator] But to actually
override the executive branch,

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.860
they would need a 2/3 majority vote.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.290
However, not everyone wants
to take the debate away

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:29.820
from Congress simply

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.790
because it's crises that
make Congress actually

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:33.810
get things done.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.270
- We find ourselves at the edge
of cliffs a lot in Congress

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.890
and that seems to be one

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.700
of the only motivators to get
people to make concessions

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.130
and reach agreements.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.130
But on the debt ceiling, that
is a very high-risk strategy.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.500
- [Narrator] Many Republicans
want that high-risk strategy

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.080
to get spending cuts in
other measures they worry

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.450
they won't get under a
Democratic president.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.640
- Republicans have tried to work with,

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:57.570
reach across the aisle

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.640
and have been shut out of
many of those conversations.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.390
If we could do that, then we
wouldn't need to threaten,

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:06.240
use the threat of the debt
ceiling as a negotiating tool.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.247
- There are definitely divisions

00:05:07.247 --> 00:05:09.570
within the Republican Party
about how they see that.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:10.680
Some of them really want it,

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.950
want to go to the brink
and force these changes

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.600
that they really believe are important,

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.670
and others don't think we should be using

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:18.840
the debt ceiling that way

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.160
and would rather just take care of it.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.870
Maybe even would rather
Democrats take care of it

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.250
in the lame-duck before they have to worry

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.050
about it next year in their majority.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.450
- [Narrator] So Democrats have
about two months to decide

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:28.680
on one of these paths

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:32.100
and potentially gain
Republican support for it.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.110
Otherwise, come 2023,

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:37.102
we'll likely see an epic
debt limit debate again.

00:05:37.102 --> 00:05:39.769
(pensive music)

